According to Article 101(1), the Commission may impose on providers of general-purpose AI models fines not exceeding **3% of their annual total worldwide turnover in the preceding financial year or EUR 15,000,000, whichever is higher** (Article 101(1)).

These fines may be imposed when the Commission finds that the provider intentionally or negligently:

(a) infringed the relevant provisions of the Regulation;
(b) failed to comply with a request for a document or information, or supplied incorrect, incomplete or misleading information;
(c) failed to comply with a measure requested by the Commission;
(d) failed to make available access to the general-purpose AI model for evaluation purposes (Article 101(1)).

In fixing the amount of the fine, the Commission must have regard to the nature, gravity and duration of the infringement, taking due account of the principles of proportionality and appropriateness. The Commission must also take into account commitments made by the provider or relevant codes of practice (Article 101(1)).

The fines imposed must be effective, proportionate and dissuasive (Article 101(3)). Additionally, decisions fixing a fine may be reviewed by the Court of Justice of the European Union, which has unlimited jurisdiction to cancel, reduce or increase the fine imposed (Article 101(5)).
